Theorem 5.2 (Scott 1981, PRG-19) states that evaluation composed with the pairing of the curry of g and the constant map of σ equals g composed with the pairing of the identity and that same constant map. Theorem 5.2, abstraction case (Scott 1981, PRG-19), states that the map recovered by applying the double curry of g at a context element, then at σ, then at y, equals g at the pair of the pair of the context element and σ with y. Theorem 5.2, tuple case (Scott 1981, PRG-19), states that the map recovered from the curry of a pairing, at a context element and at x, equals the pair of the two maps recovered from the curries of the two components at that same context element and at x. Theorem 5.2, base value law (Scott 1981, PRG-19), states that the map recovered from the curry of g at a context element v, applied at x, equals g at the pair of v and x.

The numbered name theorem_52 is the conjunction of these statements. The proof is that conjunction.
